# deploy/docker/crontab.tmpl — the template render-crontab.sh renders over
# deploy/docker/crontab at every container start (design decision D5). The
# at-sign placeholders below come from config.json's `schedule`: several
# active nodes on one shared Claude account must not all fire at once, so
# each node lands on its own minute for the two heavy pipelines — a stable
# hash of NODE_NAME by default, CYCLE_MINUTE in .env to choose, drawn only
# from the minutes schedule.excluded_minutes does not rule out.
#
# @CYCLE_MINUTE@ is a comma-separated minute list, not a single minute
# (issue #248, "faster heartbeat"): the node's own minute, then every
# schedule.cycle_interval_minutes after it within the hour, each occurrence
# dropped if it lands on an excluded minute. cron's minute field accepts
# such a list natively. cycle_interval_minutes=60 collapses this back to
# the single-minute, once-per-hour shape every release before it carried.
# An idle firing stays cheap: lib/noop-skip.sh short-circuits before the
# Co-Ordinator runs when nothing a fingerprint covers has changed.
#
# @DOCTOR_MINUTE@ is schedule.doctor_offset_minutes past the node's own base
# minute (mod 60), the same jitter the review line above uses — one node's hourly
# `doctor.sh --unattended` pass (agent-ops#543) lands away from every other
# node's, and away from this node's own heavy pipelines. `doctor.sh` exits
# non-zero on a `fail` verdict, which here is routine — a misconfigured
# `Priority` field found once an hour, not a broken job — so the line ends
# `|| true`: nothing on this image reads a cron job's exit status (no
# MAILTO, no healthcheck), but a future reader of `docker compose logs`
# must not mistake supercronic's own "job failed" line for a crashed
# script. The run's actual verdict travels through the file it writes
# (state_dir/.doctor-status.json) to the dashboard, not through this exit
# status.
#
# @REVERT_RATE_MINUTE@ @REVERT_RATE_HOUR@ is schedule.revert_rate_offset_minutes
# past the node's own base minute (mod 60), the same jitter the doctor line
# above uses, but daily rather than hourly — one node's own
# `scripts/publish-revert-rate.sh` pass (agent-ops#579) runs the merged-PR
# miner over a bounded window and appends this repository's rolling-window,
# cumulative-since-baseline and stored-baseline revert-or-follow-up rates to
# `revert-rate.jsonl`, replicated fleet-wide like `log.jsonl` (never rotated,
# never excluded from the state branch). It ends `|| true` for the identical
# reason the doctor line does: nothing on this image reads a cron job's exit
# status, and a routine `not-met` reading against the stored baseline is not a
# crashed script.
#
# @TECH_DEBT_ARCHIVE_MINUTE@ @TECH_DEBT_ARCHIVE_HOUR@ is
# schedule.tech_debt_archive_offset_minutes past the node's own base minute
# (mod 60), the same jitter shape the revert-rate line above uses, daily
# rather than hourly — one node's own `scripts/publish-tech-debt-archive.sh`
# pass (agent-ops#878, D15 as revised) mirrors every `pw::type:tech-debt`-
# labelled issue, per configured repository, into the state repository's own
# git history, one JSON file per issue. It ends `|| true` for the same
# reason the revert-rate line does: nothing on this image reads a cron job's
# exit status, and a repository whose label search or archive write failed
# this run is simply retried on the next one, not a crashed script.
#
# @CHANGELOG_ROLL_MINUTE@ @CHANGELOG_ROLL_HOUR@ @CHANGELOG_ROLL_DOW@ is
# schedule.changelog_roll_offset_minutes past the node's own base minute (mod
# 60), the same jitter shape the tech-debt-archive line above uses, but
# weekly rather than daily: @CHANGELOG_ROLL_DOW@ is cron's own day-of-week
# field (schedule.changelog_roll_day_of_week, `0`-`6`, Sunday is `0`), so —
# unlike the Pipeline Monitor's own daily-within-an-hourly-line cadence below —
# the crontab line itself only ever fires on that one day and no "is this
# due" check runs at all. One node's own `scripts/roll-changelog.sh` pass
# (agent-ops#1809) runs `scripts/assemble-changelog.sh`, renames the
# resulting `## [Unreleased]` heading to today's date and opens a fresh one,
# then opens or updates the one pull request D27 (agent-ops#1804) permits to
# edit `CHANGELOG.md`. It ends `|| true` for the same reason the revert-rate
# line does: nothing on this image reads a cron job's exit status, and a
# week with nothing merged since the last roll is a no-op, not a crashed
# script.
#
# @MONITOR_MINUTE@ is schedule.monitor_offset_minutes past the node's own base
# minute (mod 60), the same jitter the doctor line above uses, and — unlike
# the review and publish lines — it fires *every* hour rather than at one.
# That is deliberate and is the whole cadence mechanism (agent-ops#1284,
# docs/MONITOR-PIPELINE-SPEC.md M4): the Pipeline Monitor is due once a day at
# schedule.monitor_hour *and* within the hour after any `pager-fired` event,
# and supercronic cannot express the second half at all — no crontab line can
# read the fleet's own log. So the line fires hourly and `monitor-cycle.sh`
# decides, standing itself down with `cause: "not-due"` on every tick that is
# neither. A not-due tick takes and releases a lock, folds the union log a
# few times, and makes no model call at all — the cost that matters. It ends
# `|| true` for the same reason the doctor line does:
# nothing on this image reads a cron job's exit status, and a stood-down tick
# is not a crashed script.
#
# @WAKE_POLL_MINUTES@ is schedule.wake_poll_minutes (requirement 54, issue
# #613): every that-many minutes, scripts/wake-poll.sh does one conditional
# GET (If-None-Match) per configured repo against a small set of endpoints
# chosen to union-cover lib/noop-skip.sh's own fingerprint table, and invokes
# agent-cycle.sh — the same entry point the @CYCLE_MINUTE@ line below uses —
# the moment any of them shows a real change rather than a `304`. This is a
# wake-up, not a second schedule: it never stops the @CYCLE_MINUTE@ line
# below, since `abandoned-drafts` and `merge-conflicts` move no forge event a
# poll can see, and a woken node takes the same lock, the same claims and the
# same back-pressure cap as a cron-fired one. It ends `|| true` for the same
# reason the doctor line does: nothing on this image reads a cron job's exit
# status, and a repository wake-poll could not reach this tick is simply
# retried next tick, not a crashed script.
#
# The line below carries no substitution token at all, so render-crontab.sh
# leaves it untouched. It is the liveness marker `scripts/node-health.sh
# --live` reads (requirement 57, issue #608): `touch`ing a file every minute
# is nearly the cheapest thing supercronic can be asked to prove it is still
# firing jobs at all, deliberately decoupled from every other job's own
# health — a wedged dashboard-launcher window or a stuck cycle must not
# read as "not live", and a job that did real work to prove liveness could
# itself hang and take the liveness signal down with it. `|| true` for the
# same reason every other line here carries it: nothing on this image reads
# a cron job's exit status, and a `touch` failing (a full disk, a volume
# gone read-only) is a fact `node_health_liveness`'s own aged-out reading
# already reports, not a crashed script to double-report.
#
# @RESOURCE_SAMPLE_MINUTES@ is schedule.resource_sample_minutes (requirement
# 55, D14, issue #606): every that-many minutes,
# scripts/collect-resource-usage.sh takes one cheap cgroup/proc reading of
# this container's own CPU, memory and network counters, appending it to
# this node's local resource-usage log — with its own internal
# resources.disk_sample_interval_minutes throttle for the `du` reads, which
# are not cheap. Runs only inside `scheduler` (this line); `dashboard`/
# `dashboard-local` run no crontab at all, so `scripts/serve-dashboard.sh`
# starts a small background loop calling the same script on the same
# interval instead. It ends `|| true` for the same reason the doctor line
# does: nothing on this image reads a cron job's exit status, and a tick
# that could not read one counter this time is simply retried next tick,
# not a crashed script.
#
# @LOGDIR@ is config.json's own `state_dir`, expanded (`~` against `$HOME`)
# exactly as every other script that reads that key expands it: this file's
# every log redirection resolves against whatever the operator actually
# configured, never the baked default the image happens to ship.
#
# Everything not a placeholder is documented in the baked crontab, which is
# also the fallback whenever this template cannot be rendered.

LOGDIR=@LOGDIR@

* * * * *  touch $LOGDIR/.node-alive 2>/dev/null || true
*/@HEARTBEAT_MINUTES@ * * * *  /app/scripts/publish-dashboard-launcher.sh
*/@WAKE_POLL_MINUTES@ * * * *  /app/scripts/wake-poll.sh >> $LOGDIR/wake-poll.log 2>&1 || true
*/@RESOURCE_SAMPLE_MINUTES@ * * * *  /app/scripts/collect-resource-usage.sh >> $LOGDIR/resource-usage.log 2>&1 || true
@CYCLE_MINUTE@ @CYCLE_HOURS@ * * *  /app/agent-cycle.sh  >> $LOGDIR/cron.log        2>&1
@REVIEW_MINUTE@ @REVIEW_HOUR@ * * *  /app/review-cycle.sh >> $LOGDIR/review-cron.log 2>&1
@DOCTOR_MINUTE@ * * * *  /app/scripts/doctor.sh --unattended >> $LOGDIR/doctor.log 2>&1 || true
@MONITOR_MINUTE@ * * * *  /app/monitor-cycle.sh >> $LOGDIR/monitor-cron.log 2>&1 || true
@REVERT_RATE_MINUTE@ @REVERT_RATE_HOUR@ * * *  /app/scripts/publish-revert-rate.sh >> $LOGDIR/revert-rate.log 2>&1 || true
@TECH_DEBT_ARCHIVE_MINUTE@ @TECH_DEBT_ARCHIVE_HOUR@ * * *  /app/scripts/publish-tech-debt-archive.sh >> $LOGDIR/tech-debt-archive.log 2>&1 || true
@CHANGELOG_ROLL_MINUTE@ @CHANGELOG_ROLL_HOUR@ * * @CHANGELOG_ROLL_DOW@  /app/scripts/roll-changelog.sh >> $LOGDIR/changelog-roll.log 2>&1 || true

*/@STATE_SYNC_PUSH_MINUTES@ * * * *  /app/scripts/state-sync.sh push  >> $LOGDIR/state-sync.log 2>&1
*/@STATE_SYNC_FETCH_MINUTES@ * * * *  /app/scripts/state-sync.sh fetch >> $LOGDIR/state-sync.log 2>&1

@LOG_ROTATION_MINUTE@ * * * *  /app/scripts/rotate-logs.sh >> $LOGDIR/dashboard.log 2>&1
